t common divisor (GCD) of 100 and 150.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of 100 and 150:\n\\[\n100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\\[\n150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor common to both numbers:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2^1 \\times 5^2 = 50\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"2d2354c0c449ab7d1d837793146147a492f702c8a412f724d5c9df2dad98a377"},"seconds":6.881951570510864,"seed":912305161607944673,"stop":"eos","text":"Now that we have the GCD of 100 and 150 as 50, we need to identify all the positive two-digit factors of 50. The factors of 50 are 1, 2, 5, 10, 25, and 50. Among these, the only two-digit factor is 50 itself.\n\nTherefore, the number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is:\n\n\\boxed{1}"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"Now that we have the GCD of 100 and 150 as 50, we need to identify all the positive two-digit factors of 50. The factors of 50 are 1, 2, 5, 10, 25, and 50. Among these, the only two-digit factor is 50 itself.\n\nTherefore, the number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is:\n\n\\boxed{1}","id":"prealgebra:567"}
